The pitch for background jobs is simple: slow work should not block a response.
Move it to a queue, return immediately, everyone is happier.

That is true, and it undersells the cost. A queue turns one synchronous
operation with one outcome into a distributed system with at-least-once
delivery, partial failure, and ordering you no longer control. Worth it — often —
but not free, and the decision deserves more than "this endpoint feels slow".

## The rule

Work belongs on a queue when **the caller does not need the result to proceed,
and the work can be safely repeated.**

Both halves matter.

If the caller needs the result, a queue does not remove the wait — it relocates
it into polling or a socket subscription, and now you have the latency *and* the
infrastructure. If the work cannot be safely repeated, a queue is actively
dangerous, because at-least-once delivery means it will eventually run twice.

Applied to what we had:

| Work | Queue? | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Payroll run for 400 employees | Yes | Nobody watches it complete; idempotent per period |
| Report generation | Yes | Result is fetched later; regenerating is harmless |
| Bulk CSV import | Yes | Long, chunkable, resumable |
| Sending one transactional email | Yes | Fire-and-forget, retry-safe with a dedupe key |
| Charging a card | **No** | Caller needs the outcome; repeating is not harmless |
| Validating a form | No | Caller needs it immediately |

The card row is the one people get wrong. It is slow and it calls an external
API, so it pattern-matches to "queue this". But the user is standing there
waiting to know whether their payment worked, and a retried charge is a real
financial event. Slow and synchronous is the correct answer.

## Idempotency is the price of admission

Every queue is at-least-once. Not "usually once" — a worker can complete the work
and then die before acknowledging, and the job will be redelivered. Plan for it.

```ts
// jobId makes enqueueing idempotent: BullMQ will not add a second job with
// the same id while one is active or waiting.
await payrollQueue.add(
  "run-payroll",
  { tenantId, periodId },
  {
    jobId: `payroll:${tenantId}:${periodId}`,
    attempts: 5,
    backoff: { type: "exponential", delay: 2_000 },
    removeOnComplete: { age: 86_400, count: 1_000 },
    removeOnFail: { age: 604_800 },
  },
);
```

`jobId` handles duplicate *enqueues*. It does not handle duplicate *execution* —
a job that fails on attempt three after doing half its work will re-run from the
start. So the handler has to be idempotent too:

```ts
worker.process("run-payroll", async (job) => {
  const { tenantId, periodId } = job.data;

  // Claim the period. If another attempt already finished it, stop.
  const claimed = await db.payrollRun.updateMany({
    where: { tenantId, periodId, status: "PENDING" },
    data: { status: "RUNNING", startedAt: new Date() },
  });

  if (claimed.count === 0) {
    const existing = await db.payrollRun.findUnique({
      where: { tenantId_periodId: { tenantId, periodId } },
    });
    if (existing?.status === "COMPLETE") return existing;
    // RUNNING but not ours — another worker has it.
    throw new Error("payroll run already in progress");
  }

  // ... do the work, writing per-employee rows with a unique constraint
  // on (periodId, employeeId) so partial re-runs cannot double-pay.
});
```

The unique constraint is doing the real work there. Application-level "have I
done this already?" checks race. A database constraint does not.

## Set removeOnComplete, on day one

Redis holds completed jobs until told otherwise. A queue processing a few
thousand jobs a day will quietly consume all available memory over a few weeks,
and Redis running out of memory takes down everything else using it.

Set `removeOnComplete` and `removeOnFail` when you create the queue, not after
the incident.

## Concurrency is per-worker, and that surprises people

```ts
new Worker("payroll", handler, { concurrency: 5 });
```

That is five concurrent jobs *per worker process*. Run four instances and you
have twenty, which is a good way to discover your database connection pool
limit at three in the morning. Reason about total concurrency across the fleet,
not per process.

For shared infrastructure — a rate-limited external API, or one tenant's bulk
import that should not starve everyone else — use a rate limiter or a group key
rather than tuning concurrency:

```ts
new Worker("carrier-sync", handler, {
  concurrency: 10,
  limiter: { max: 20, duration: 1_000 }, // 20/sec across this worker
});
```

## Failures need somewhere to go

A job that exhausts its attempts should not vanish into a log line. Move it
somewhere a human will look:

```ts
worker.on("failed", async (job, error) => {
  if (job && job.attemptsMade >= (job.opts.attempts ?? 1)) {
    await alertOnCall({
      queue: job.queueName,
      jobId: job.id,
      data: job.data,
      error: error.message,
    });
  }
});
```

Without this, the failure mode is silent. Payroll did not run, nobody was told,
and you find out from the client. Ask me how I know.

## What we actually got

Moving payroll runs, report generation, and bulk imports off the request path
improved perceived API responsiveness by about 40%. That number is honest but
narrow — it is p95 latency on the endpoints that previously did the work inline,
not a system-wide improvement. The work still takes as long; it just happens
where nobody is waiting.

What we also got: two new failure modes (jobs stuck in `RUNNING` after a worker
died, and one duplicate-execution bug that the unique constraint caught before
it mattered), a Redis instance that is now production-critical, and a dashboard
somebody has to look at.

That trade was clearly worth it for this workload. It would not have been worth
it to make a 200ms endpoint into a 50ms endpoint. The threshold, roughly, is
whether the work is slow enough that a human notices — and whether you are
prepared to own a distributed system to fix it.
